// Reading genomes. Generated by scripts/generate (see .claude/skills/generate-place/SKILL.md).
// Blocks between <generated:…> markers are written by scripts; everything else is drafted.
// Arc: a puzzle (where do falcons belong?), what a genome is and how big, how genomes are read
// and compared letter by letter, counting the differences yourself, then new situations.
// Hero: a peregrine falcon (USFWS, public domain). Sim: the sequence archetype (sequenceLab):
// real mitochondrial letters from NCBI RefSeq, lined up and counted; only after the counts does an
// induced subtree of the Open Tree of Life appear, as the reveal (content/generator/genetics-genomes/otol-trees.json).
// Proof is counting, never reading a tree: every case is a sequence case whose winning pair is the
// pair whose letters differ least, computed by the engine, and no case draws a tree.
//
// Why these letters: short stretches of mitochondrial DNA are saturated at the depth of the falcon–
// parrot split (in COX1 the falcon's letters sit nearer the eagle's), so no stretch here pretends to
// show that, and no learner-facing line says a count of letters showed it: it comes from whole-genome
// studies (Jarvis 2014) and is drawn as the reveal. Every comparison used is shallow enough that the
// whole gene agrees with the window on the closest pair (whole-gene p-distances, Needleman–Wunsch,
// match +1 / mismatch −1 / gap −2, 2026-10-26): falcons COX3 10.2% vs 12.9–19.1%; thylacine 16S
// 13.4% vs 14.9–21.3%; puma ND1 7.4% vs 10.3–11.8%; wolverine ND2 12.2% vs 16.1–22.3%. For the
// puma and the wolverine the whole gene also orders every other pair the way the Open Tree does.
// Each case window orders every other pair the way the Open Tree does, so the counts printed after
// a run never contradict the reveal.

